   "title": "Korea, US kick off combined air drills this week",
   "url": "https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/southkorea/defense/20261026/korea-us-kick-off-combined-air-drills-this-week",
   "domain": "koreatimes.co.kr",
   "source": "koreatimes.co.kr",
   "published": "Mon, 26 Oct 2026 05:32:03 GMT",
   "_dt": 1792992723.0,
   "ageDays": 0.52,
   "summary": "Korea and the United States kicked off their combined air drills this week, focusing on counter-air and air interdiction missions aimed at enhancing wartime joint operations, the Air Force said Monday. Reflecting the changing modern warfare environment, the drills will also evaluate the planning and execution of Korea-led combined exercises in line with the ongoing efforts for Seoul's retaking of wartime operational control (OPCON) from Washington, the Air Force noted.",
   "summary_zh": "韩国和美国本周启动了联合空中演习，重点演练反空袭和空中拦截任务，以增强战时联合行动能力。韩国空军周一表示，此次演习反映现代战争环境的变化，还将评估韩国主导的联合演习的计划与执行情况，与首尔继续努力从华盛顿收回战时作战指挥权（OPCON）的举措相衔接。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Korea and the United States kicked off their combined air drills this week, focusing on counter-air and air interdiction missions aimed at enhancing wartime joint operations, the Air Force said Monday. The Freedom Flag exercise began Monday for a two-week run at Kunsan Air Base in Gunsan, about 180 kilometers southwest of Seoul, involving the allies' key aerial assets, including the Korean Air Force's F-15K, KF-16 and F-35A fighter jets, the armed force said in a release. The KC-330 transport aircraft and E-737 Peace Eye early warning and control aircraft will also be deployed for the exercise. The U.S. Air Force's F-16 Fighting Falcon jet, RC-135 reconnaissance plane and its Navy's MH-60S Seahawk helicopter, as well as the F-35B stealth fighter jet and the MQ-9 Reaper unmanned surveillance aircraft of the U.S. Marines Corps will also participate in the drills. The air forces will operate day and nighttime \"mission planning cells\" to exchange tactics and expertise, while refining wartime operational and mission execution procedures, the Air Force said. The missions are designed to enhance interoperability and build wartime combined operational capabilities, such as defensive counter-air and air interdiction, it said. Reflecting the changing modern warfare environment, the drills will also evaluate the planning and execution of Korea-led combined exercises in line with the ongoing efforts for Seoul's retaking of wartime operational control (OPCON) from Washington, the Air Force noted. Korea seeks to retake wartime OPCON from the U.S. by 2030 or at an earlier date by verifying its capabilities to lead the combined forces. The semiannual Freedom Flag drills were launched in 2024 to replace regular large-scale air exercises between the allies — the Korea Flying Training in the first half of the year and Vigilant Defense in the second half",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
