   "title": "CHINA DEMANDS A MASSIVE PRICE FROM PUTIN FOR HELPING END THE WAR IN UKRAINE — BEIJING WANTS ALMOST UNLIMITED ACCESS TO RUSSIA’S NATURAL RESO",
   "url": "https://x.com/OlenaRohoza/status/2113920186709426296",
   "snippet": "CHINA DEMANDS A MASSIVE PRICE FROM PUTIN FOR HELPING END THE WAR IN UKRAINE — BEIJING WANTS ALMOST UNLIMITED ACCESS TO RUSSIA’S NATURAL RESOURCES IN EXCHANGE FOR GUARANTEES OF A PEACE DEAL WITH TRUMP.\n\nAccording to alleged Kremlin insiders, Xi Jinping is prepared to become the main international guarantor of a future peace agreement on Ukraine. However, Chinese leaders have reportedly presented Moscow with a series of economic demands.\n\nBeijing wants to turn its participation in the peace negotiations into long-term advantages for Chinese businesses. The Kremlin is reportedly discussing just how many concessions it will have to make to secure Xi’s support in negotiations with Trump.\n\nChina is primarily interested in deposits of rare-earth metals, copper, gold, and other natural resources in Siberia and the Russian Far East.\n\nBeijing is reportedly demanding priority access for Chinese companies to major extraction projects, long-term contracts for raw materials at preferential prices, and special privileges for Chinese investors. Expanding Chinese involvement in Russia’s forestry and energy industries is also reportedly under discussion.\n\nChina wants these agreements finalized before any official announcement ending Russia’s war against Ukraine.\n\nBeijing fears that once American sanctions are potentially lifted, Russia could resume extensive cooperation with Western companies, threatening the privileged position Chinese investors have gained in recent years.\n\nXi therefore wants binding guarantees that would remain in force regardless of any future rapprochement between Moscow and Washington.\n\nFor Putin, this creates a serious dilemma.\n\nOn one hand, China's participation could significantly increase the chances of reaching an agreement with Trump to end the fighting and gradually lift sanctions.\n\nOn the other hand, accepting Beijing’s demands could mean that a substantial share of Russia’s future natural-resource revenues would effectively be tied to Chinese interests for decades.\n\nIn other words, Putin may have to mortgage Russia’s natural wealth to China in exchange for a way out of the war he started.",
   "body": "CHINA DEMANDS A MASSIVE PRICE FROM PUTIN FOR HELPING END THE WAR IN UKRAINE — BEIJING WANTS ALMOST UNLIMITED ACCESS TO RUSSIA’S NATURAL RESOURCES IN EXCHANGE FOR GUARANTEES OF A PEACE DEAL WITH TRUMP.\n\nAccording to alleged Kremlin insiders, Xi Jinping is prepared to become the main international guarantor of a future peace agreement on Ukraine. However, Chinese leaders have reportedly presented Moscow with a series of economic demands.\n\nBeijing wants to turn its participation in the peace negotiations into long-term advantages for Chinese businesses. The Kremlin is reportedly discussing just how many concessions it will have to make to secure Xi’s support in negotiations with Trump.\n\nChina is primarily interested in deposits of rare-earth metals, copper, gold, and other natural resources in Siberia and the Russian Far East.\n\nBeijing is reportedly demanding priority access for Chinese companies to major extraction projects, long-term contracts for raw materials at preferential prices, and special privileges for Chinese investors. Expanding Chinese involvement in Russia’s forestry and energy industries is also reportedly under discussion.\n\nChina wants these agreements finalized before any official announcement ending Russia’s war against Ukraine.\n\nBeijing fears that once American sanctions are potentially lifted, Russia could resume extensive cooperation with Western companies, threatening the privileged position Chinese investors have gained in recent years.\n\nXi therefore wants binding guarantees that would remain in force regardless of any future rapprochement between Moscow and Washington.\n\nFor Putin, this creates a serious dilemma.\n\nOn one hand, China's participation could significantly increase the chances of reaching an agreement with Trump to end the fighting and gradually lift sanctions.\n\nOn the other hand, accepting Beijing’s demands could mean that a substantial share of Russia’s future natural-resource revenues would effectively be tied to Chinese interests for decades.\n\nIn other words, Putin may have to mortgage Russia’s natural wealth to China in exchange for a way out of the war he started.",
